# Backend Philosophy

Supabase is the primary backend platform for the MVP.

The backend architecture must prioritize:
- fast iteration
- simplicity
- realtime capabilities
- maintainability
- predictable data flows

Avoid:
- unnecessary backend infrastructure
- premature microservices
- complex event-driven systems
- excessive backend abstractions

# Supabase Responsibilities

Supabase handles:
- authentication
- database
- realtime
- storage
- authorization
- lightweight backend logic

Avoid introducing custom backend services unless absolutely necessary.

# Database Philosophy

Use PostgreSQL relational modeling.

Prioritize:
- simplicity
- consistency
- maintainability
- predictable schemas

Avoid:
- overengineered database designs
- unnecessary normalization complexity
- premature scalability patterns

# Table Design Rules

- Use UUID primary keys
- Always include timestamps
- Use foreign keys correctly
- Keep naming consistent
- Prefer explicit relations

Preferred columns:

id uuid primary key
created_at timestamptz
updated_at timestamptz

# Naming Conventions

Use snake_case for:
- tables
- columns
- foreign keys

Good:
- user_profiles
- created_at
- job_applications

Avoid:
- camelCase columns
- inconsistent naming
- abbreviations

# Relationship Rules

Prefer relational modeling.

Good:
- foreign keys
- normalized relationships
- explicit ownership

Avoid:
- duplicated relational data
- denormalized hacks without justification

# Realtime Philosophy

Use Supabase Realtime only where valuable.

Good realtime use cases:
- chat
- live availability
- ride/job updates
- presence systems
- realtime statuses

Avoid:
- realtime for everything
- unnecessary subscriptions
- excessive client synchronization

# Realtime Rules

- Keep subscriptions scoped
- Clean up subscriptions properly
- Avoid duplicate subscriptions
- Keep realtime flows predictable

Prefer:
- lightweight event handling
- query invalidation
- simple synchronization logic

# Authentication Rules

Use Supabase Auth exclusively.

Rules:
- persist sessions securely
- centralize auth logic
- protect authenticated routes
- avoid duplicated auth state

# Authorization Rules

Always use Row Level Security.

Rules:
- every table must define policies
- never trust client-side validation alone
- keep policies explicit and predictable

Avoid:
- public unrestricted tables
- overly permissive policies
- hidden authorization logic

# Security Standards

- Never expose service role keys
- Use environment variables correctly
- Validate user ownership properly
- Protect sensitive operations

# Environment Variables

Use:

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

Never hardcode credentials.

# Query Rules

- Keep queries simple
- Prefer reusable services
- Avoid duplicated query logic
- Select only required fields

Avoid:
- massive nested queries
- overfetching
- duplicated transformations

# Service Layer Rules

All Supabase communication belongs inside services.

Good:

features/jobs/services/jobs.service.ts

Avoid:
- direct Supabase logic everywhere
- duplicated queries across components

# Storage Rules

Use Supabase Storage for:
- profile images
- uploaded documents
- lightweight media

Rules:
- validate uploads
- keep bucket structure organized
- avoid oversized uploads unnecessarily

# Edge Functions

Use Edge Functions only when necessary.

Good use cases:
- secure server-side logic
- third-party integrations
- protected workflows

Avoid:
- moving all logic to edge functions
- unnecessary backend complexity

# Error Handling

- Always handle backend errors
- Use user-friendly messages
- Avoid exposing raw database errors
- Keep async flows predictable

# Realtime + TanStack Query

Preferred integration:
- lightweight realtime events
- query invalidation
- predictable cache updates

Avoid:
- excessive manual reconciliation
- giant realtime state systems

# MVP Philosophy

The backend should remain:
- simple
- reliable
- fast to iterate
- easy to debug

Prioritize:
- shipping product
- validating ideas
- maintaining velocity

Over:
- enterprise backend complexity
- premature scalability
- unnecessary infrastructure

# Cursor AI Expectations

When generating Supabase logic:
- Use relational modeling
- Respect RLS policies
- Keep schemas maintainable
- Generate reusable services
- Avoid backend overengineering
- Prefer simple predictable queries
- Use realtime only when valuable